WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.067
Graph neural networks.

2
00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:07.567
Some data's structure is the signal, and flattening it into rows throws that signal away.

3
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:12.133
Think about citations, transactions, and chemical bonds.

4
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:13.433
Who cites whom.

5
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:15.000
Who transacts with whom.

6
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.372
Which atoms bond.

7
00:00:16.372 --> 00:00:21.167
Flatten that into rows, and the fraud ring or research community disappears.

8
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:23.633
Here's a tiny graph.

9
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.000
Four nodes, which could be papers, accounts, or atoms.

10
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.000
The edges between them carry the pattern a GNN learns from.

11
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.553
A GNN learns through message passing.

12
00:00:34.553 --> 00:00:36.333
Each layer does four things.

13
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:40.033
Every node gathers its neighbors' current representations.

14
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:43.700
It aggregates them, with a sum, a mean, or attention.

15
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.233
It combines the result with its own vector.

16
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:49.267
And that gives its updated representation.

17
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:53.433
Stack L layers, and each node summarizes its L-hop neighborhood.

18
00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:57.200
The embeddings feed ordinary prediction heads.

19
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.367
Classify nodes: is this account fraudulent?

20
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:03.400
Predict links: will these papers be co-cited?

21
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.000
Or classify whole graphs: is this molecule toxic?

22
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.733
PyTorch Geometric is the standard library, and Cora is its MNIST.

23
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:17.200
Cora is a citation network of two thousand seven hundred eight papers.

24
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:23.400
The first layer takes fourteen thirty three word features in, and the second outputs seven topic classes.

25
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.067
Two layers means each paper sees two hops of its neighborhood.

26
00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:29.500
Here's a real use.

27
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.000
Your Mission Control reports cite each other, but the RAG never uses that.

28
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.367
A two-layer GCN classifies which program a report belongs to.

29
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:46.433
It gets eighty four percent, against sixty nine for a text-only classifier, because the neighborhood betrays the program.

30
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:52.600
Bonus: chunks from reports one hop away make good expansion candidates at retrieval time.

31
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:54.584
Let's recap.

32
00:01:54.584 --> 00:01:57.067
Some data's signal lives in its edges.

33
00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:00.467
Message passing gathers, aggregates, and combines.

34
00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:02.733
L layers see the L-hop neighborhood.

35
00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:06.413
And heads can predict nodes, links, or whole graphs.

36
00:02:06.413 --> 00:02:08.233
Next up: federated learning.
